     "title": "Write and Commit to Your 90-Day Plan",
     "content": "Complete all six components for your own real context, using the previous five lessons as your source material: your top two or three armor triggers and named replacements from the contrast-list audit, your BRAVING debrief cadence, your SFD rule for charged communications, your pre-written self-compassion script, and one weekly innovation-loop behavior. For each component, write the specific indicator you will track and the exact date you will first check it - thirty days out is a reasonable first checkpoint for behaviors, ninety days for culture-level shifts like BRAVING scores.\n\nIdentify one accountability structure that does not depend on your own willpower alone - a colleague who has agreed to name your armor pattern when they see it, a standing calendar hold for the BRAVING debrief that isn't yours to cancel unilaterally, or a team ritual that runs whether or not you personally remember to initiate it. Willpower is the least reliable part of any behavior-change plan, and external structure is what catches the weeks willpower doesn't show up.\n\nSet a firm date, ninety days out, to rewrite this entire plan rather than simply renew it. Armor finds new triggers as leaders grow and take on new responsibilities, and a plan calibrated to this year's stressors will quietly go stale against next year's. Treat the rewrite itself as the recurring practice - daring leadership development, in Brown's framing, is not a plan you finish, it is a plan you keep rebuilding.",
